WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.875
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi,
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,

00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:04.200
and this is AI
Simplified, where we

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.420
learn to make your data useful.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.340
In the last few
episodes, we have

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.100
been covering different
components of Vertex AI

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.680
to get you familiar
with different features.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.800
In this video, I thought we
would help a friend of mine

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.820
get into yoga.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.000
So we are building a yoga
pose Classification Model

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.490
for some simple yoga poses.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.640
This will help her
hone her yoga skills.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.760
First of all, let's see how
we will make this happen.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.850
We need the data to
create our models with.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.070
This would be in
the form of images,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.290
and let's say we
want to focus on five

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.040
poses, the Tree, the Goddess,
the Warrior II, Plank,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.240
and Down Dog.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.300
My colleague Sara and I have
collected a bunch of images

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.950
from a yoga practice
that we will

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.000
be using to train this model.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.290
Our first step would be to
ingest these images using

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:56.910
the Manage Data Sets, and
then label them appropriately.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.830
Then comes training the model.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.280
And since this is an image
classification problem,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.680
it's a good fit for AutoML.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.030
And as we covered
in the last episode,

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.510
you can totally create a
custom model from scratch

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.940
if you wanted to,
but AutoML is just

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.120
the right fit for this
particular Image Classification

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.530
problem.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.780
Once our model is
trained, we will

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.710
evaluate it to understand
the quality of our model

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.180
and indicate areas
of improvement.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.540
Now, AutoML UI
provides us with easy

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.360
to understand charts for
this that we will demystify

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.190
when I show you the console.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.100
Then, we will need
an endpoint to serve

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.260
our models for predictions.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.630
After training our
AutoML model, we

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.710
will be able to deploy an
endpoint with just one click.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.410
Now that we have laid
out the data requirements

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.540
and how we will
train our model, we

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.050
are ready to jump into the
console and make it happen.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.810
First thing we need is the data.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.940
For this step, my
friend Sara and I

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.460
collected a bunch of
images from a yoga practice

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:02.760
and uploaded them into
the Manage Data Sets.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.450
As you see here, the images
are labeled in five categories.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.320
I can even upload unlabeled
images and label them.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.240
We can analyze our
data set to see

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.050
that we have enough
examples of each categories.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.900
Now, we are ready
to train our model.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.620
In here, I'm selecting
AutoML for training method.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.290
You can also train a model
to deploy on the Edge

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.320
or on-premise, or use custom
training if you're writing

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.990
your own Custom Model Code.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.510
Now, we define our model.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.670
AutoML automatically
splits the data

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.950
into Training,
Validation, and Test,

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.610
but we can change
it if you want to.

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.600
In Compute and pricing, we get
to select how many node hours

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.030
we want to train the model.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.390
At this point, we wait
for the model to train.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.030
Once it is trained, we
see it in the Model tab

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:58.330
and can evaluate the model.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.670
We can see our model's
Average precision.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:05.350
It ranges from 0 to 1, where a
higher value indicates a higher

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.170
quality model.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:09.970
We can see that the
Confusion matrix

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.780
helps us understand where our
model is confusing to results.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.730
Now that we have
evaluated our model,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.120
it's time to deploy
it to an endpoint.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.940
We can split traffic
between two models deployed

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.170
on the same endpoint, if needed.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.780
Allocate the number of nodes
needed for this endpoint.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.730
Depending on where
your users are,

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.550
select a region to
deploy your endpoint.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:37.120
And that's it, hit Deploy,
and we have an endpoint.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.640
Now we can test our
endpoint and the model

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.230
by uploading an image.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.000
And yay, it works.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.020
You can also make
predictions using the SDK.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.100
All right, so today we created
a yoga pose Classification

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.460
Model where we uploaded
our data sets in Vertex AI,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.460
labeled our images in
the UI, and then used

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.440
AutoML to train our model.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.380
Then we evaluated it
and then deployed it

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.110
for making predictions
with just one click.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.210
I know my friend
will be extremely

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.780
happy to use this model
to hone her yoga skills.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.730
Stay tuned for the
next episode, which

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:15.730
is all about using
what Vertex AI SDK.

